; `def` with a new symbol as an argument of an existing operator is rejected (the 2026-10-07
; review found a diagonal contradiction even for an unused operator). The 2026-10-06 review found: with
; `plus` bound to the calculator's `add`, `def c plus $x = 0` was accepted, and then
;     c plus 0 = 0,  c plus 1 = 0,  c = 0 (calc),  0 plus 1 = 0,  1 = 0 (calc),  false
; -- a complete proof of `false` without `use` or `todo`. Now the `def` is refused.
load numbers
const plus
infix plus 60 60
calc plus add
expect "EvalError"
    def c plus $x = 0

; so are the other ways to give an operator a meaning without a fact
const tt
infix tt 60 60
flat tt
expect "EvalError"
    def d tt $x = 0
expect "EvalError"
    def e + $x = 0

; even an operator without earlier meaning is not safe here (a diagonal RHS can contradict it)
const rel
infix rel 50 50
expect "EvalError"
    def p rel $x iff $x = $x

; an ordinary fresh predicate is supported
def p($x) iff $x = $x
p(3)
